    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "If ${16}^{2a}=\\frac{1}{{2}^{a-2}}$ then $a=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $16$ as ${2}^{4},$ and then we rewrite the right-hand side using the power rule of exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{16}^{2a} & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{a-2}} \\\\ {({2}^{4})}^{2a} & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{a-2}} \\\\ {2}^{8a} & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{a-2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using a negative exponent: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{8a} & ={2}^{-(a-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(2),$ we equate the exponents and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8a & =-(a-2) \\\\ 8a & =-a+2 \\\\ 9a & =2 \\\\ a & =\\frac{2}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/id": "cdd837a2-e461-5885-8a33-ef3209361d98"
